# Lines tools/qemu-test must see, in this order, as whole serial lines.
# In QEMU: usb-test's controller lines; then
# usb0 enumerates QEMU's USB keyboard, mouse and tablet on its root ports 5
# to 7 - each device's vendor, product, class, USB release and speed, then
# each boot interface ready, and the mouse's and tablet's layouts read from
# their report descriptors: the mouse has a wheel, so it is in the report
# protocol - and once usb-input-test.qmp types, moves and clicks, their
# reports reach the UI's stand-in as canonical events through the input
# broker. The keyboard is unplugged with "c" held - its release comes
# with it - and plugged back: enumerated afresh, its keys arrive again.
# Then it is unplugged and plugged straight back, with no wait between: the
# connection change is read as an unplug, and it is enumerated again. Each
# device's first report is logged by its length alone, never its contents.
# Last the wheel turns: the tablet's, then, the tablet unplugged, the
# mouse's.
NOWAYOS: usb0: starting
NOWAYOS: usb0: controller identified
NOWAYOS: usb0: halted and reset
NOWAYOS: usb0: running: a No Op command came back through DMA
NOWAYOS: usb0: ready
NOWAYOS: usb1: starting
NOWAYOS: usb1: controller identified
NOWAYOS: usb1: halted and reset
NOWAYOS: usb1: running: a No Op command came back through DMA
NOWAYOS: usb1: ready
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 5: device 0627:0001, class 0/0/0, USB 2.0, high-speed
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 5: boot keyboard ready
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 6: device 0627:0001, class 0/0/0, USB 2.0, high-speed
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 6: mouse ready, report protocol: report ID 0; X at bit 8, 8 bits, -127 to 127; Y at bit 16, 8 bits, -127 to 127
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 6: its wheel at bit 24, 8 bits, -127 to 127
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 7: device 0627:0001, class 0/0/0, USB 2.0, high-speed
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 7: tablet ready: report ID 0; X at bit 8, 16 bits, 0 to 32767; Y at bit 24, 16 bits, 0 to 32767
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 5: the keyboard's first report: 8 bytes
NOWAYOS: input-print: key down 0x04
NOWAYOS: input-print: key up 0x04
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 6: the mouse's first report: 4 bytes
NOWAYOS: input-print: pointer at (410, 235)
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 7: the tablet's first report: 6 bytes
NOWAYOS: input-print: pointer at (399, 239)
NOWAYOS: input-print: button 0 down
NOWAYOS: input-print: button 0 up
NOWAYOS: input-print: key down 0x06
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 5: unplugged
NOWAYOS: input-print: key up 0x06
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 5: device 0627:0001, class 0/0/0, USB 2.0, high-speed
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 5: boot keyboard ready
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 5: the keyboard's first report: 8 bytes
NOWAYOS: input-print: key down 0x05
NOWAYOS: input-print: key up 0x05
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 5: unplugged
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 5: device 0627:0001, class 0/0/0, USB 2.0, high-speed
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 5: boot keyboard ready
NOWAYOS: input-print: key down 0x07
NOWAYOS: input-print: key up 0x07
# A held key repeats: its press, two repeats, its release.
NOWAYOS: input-print: key down 0x07
NOWAYOS: input-print: key down 0x07
NOWAYOS: input-print: key down 0x07
NOWAYOS: input-print: key up 0x07
# The wheel: the tablet's, then the mouse's.
NOWAYOS: input-print: wheel +1
NOWAYOS: usb0: port 7: unplugged
NOWAYOS: input-print: wheel -1

# Output that fails the test at once if it appears anywhere (substring match):
# a fault reported by the Microkit monitor, a kernel error message, a panic,
# a PD giving up, an event or record refused, a device left alone, or an
# event dropped.
!MON|ERROR
!seL4(CPU
!panicked
!gave up
!refused
!left alone
!dropped so far
